l these other  candidates for  the good what about pleasure  what about peace of mind although those  sorts of things  so kant is not saying that we can't ever  think of other things and understand  them to be good he's saying if we if we  completely think it through from his  perspective  the only thing that then we can conceive  of as being  truly good  is the good will so how can this be well  he looks at a number of different  candidates at the beginning  another bunch of other possibilities for  what could be good  and he says why they're not actually the  good  so talents of mind what do we mean by  this he gives you a few examples  wit is among  them by which he doesn't mean just  being witty being funny or anything like  that but being able to connect things  together  intelligence being able to think things  through  being able to draw conclusions being  able to reflect on things  judgment  the capacity to  to judge well when it comes to difficult  matters  those are good things  but like he says  these can be extremely bad and hurtful  when the will is not good which is going  to make use of these so that's a  really central thing the will itself  is what decides to  make use of these it decides what's  going to happen with them  so great intelligence for example can be  used for evil as well as for for good  wit even in the sense of humor can be  used to  bring a community together it can be  used to tear people down  judgment you know figuring out precisely  the right place to hit the person so as  to cause the greatest pain that could be  a matter of judgment but that doesn't  sound like something that we'd always  say is you know representative of the  good will that's more like something a  sadist would do isn't it  so talents of mind are not going to  be the the pri the ultimate good or the  primal good  what about  qualities of  temperament  when we talk about somebody's character  their personality the way that they are  kant has some examples of t